## ASP.NET Framework → ASP.NET Core 8 Migration

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Response.End()` / `Response.Redirect(..., true)`** — These relied on `Thread.Abort`, which **does not exist** in .NET Core. There is no `ThreadAbortException`.
2. **WebForms event model** (`btnExport_Click`) — Gone entirely. Use MVC actions or Minimal APIs.
3. **`Response.Clear()` / `Response.AddHeader()`** — Replaced by the `Results` / `FileResult` API.
4. **`Response.Write()`** — Replaced by returning result objects from actions.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
// ReportController.cs — ASP.NET Core 8 MVC
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // POST /Report/Export
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        // File() sets Content-Disposition and ContentType for you.
        // No Response.End() needed — returning terminates the pipeline cleanly.
        var data = GenerateReport();
        var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);
        return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
    }

    // POST /Report/Redirect
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult RedirectAfterProcess()
    {
        // User.Identity works the same, but auth is middleware-driven now.
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // Redirect() returns a 302 — no thread abort, no exception.
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // NOTE: Do NOT catch ThreadAbortException — it doesn't exist in .NET Core.
        // That entire pattern is gone. Redirect is just a return value now.
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }

    private string GenerateReport() => /* your logic */;
    private void ProcessRequest() => /* your logic */;
    private void LogError(Exception ex) => /* your logic */;
}
```

---

### Migration Pitfalls Cheat Sheet

| Old Pattern | Core Equivalent | Pitfall |
|---|---|---|
| `Response.End()` | Just `return` from action | Calling `HttpResponse.CompleteAsync()` is **not** the same as `End()` |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | `return Redirect(url)` | Forgetting to `return` — code after `Redirect()` **keeps executing** |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | **Delete it** | `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` if called |
| `Response.Write(data)` | `return Content(data)` | Writing directly to `Response.Body` skips filters/formatters |
| `Response.AddHeader(...)` | `Response.Headers.Append(...)` | Only if you need custom headers; `File()` handles content-disposition |
| `Response.Clear()` | Not needed | Response isn't buffered the same way; headers can't be modified after body starts |
| `[Authorize]` attribute | Same name, different namespace | Must configure auth middleware in `Program.cs` |

### Critical `Program.cs` Setup

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddControllersWithViews();
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(/* your scheme */);
builder.Services.AddAuthorization();

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseAuthentication();  // Order matters — before Authorization
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();

app.Run();
```

### The #1 Gotcha

In Framework, `Response.Redirect(url, true)` **aborted the thread** — code after it never ran. In Core, `Redirect()` returns an object. **If you forget `return`:**

```csharp
// BUG: ProcessRequest() still executes after Redirect!
Redirect("/login");
ProcessRequest(); // ← this runs!

// CORRECT:
return Redirect("/login");
```

Every `Response.Redirect` in your old code must become `return Redirect(...)`. Audit every call site — this is the most common source of post-migration bugs.
